{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA1212018","title":"Impact of Electron Acceptors on Anaerobic Degradation of Benzo[a]anthracene in Urban River Sediments: A Microcosm Study","description":"Urban rivers represent typical ecosystems that are significantly impacted by various anthropogenic pollutants. To investigate the effects of different electron acceptors on the anaerobic degradation of benzo[a]anthracene (BaA) by microorganisms in urban river sediments, a microcosm culture experiment was conducted. BaA was added to urban river sediments, and electron acceptors such as nitrate (NO3-) and ferric iron (Fe(III)) were introduced. The study aimed to explore the degradation efficiency of BaA and the response of microbial community structure under different treatments. After 90 days of incubation, the concentration of BaA decreased significantly across all treatments, with no significant difference observed in degradation efficiency between NO3- and Fe(III) additions. Sequencing results indicated that Desulfosporosinus, Azoarcus, Geothermobacter, Thermoanaerobaculum, Gaiella, Ellin6067, Arenimonas, and Ilumatobacter are potential bacterial taxa involved in BaA degradation. The addition of different electron acceptors altered the composition of PAH-degrading bacteria, with NO3- promoting the growth of Geothermobacter and Azoarcus, while Fe(III) promoted Bacteroidetes_vadinHA17 and Desulfosporosinus. Gene prediction using PICRUSt2 revealed that NO3- addition significantly increased the abundance of multiple PAH degradation-related genes. This study demonstrates that the degradation of PAHs in urban river sediments is driven by diverse degrading bacteria with varying metabolic pathways, which respond to changes in available electron acceptors.","organism":null,"data_type":"Raw sequence reads","center_name":"China West Normal University","submission_date":"2025/01/17 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","cit